  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q38-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Show that $P(x)=x^3+5x-2$ has no integer roots.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The only possible integer roots are $\\pm1,\\pm2$. Since $P(1)=4$, $P(-1)=-8$, $P(2)=16$ and $P(-2)=-20$, none is a root.",
      "worked": "By the integer root theorem, any integer root must be a factor of $-2$, so test $\\pm1$ and $\\pm2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\nP(1)&=1+5-2=4\\\\\nP(-1)&=-1-5-2=-8\\\\\nP(2)&=8+10-2=16\\\\\nP(-2)&=-8-10-2=-20.\n\\end{align*}$$\nNone is zero, so $P(x)$ has no integer roots.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
